; IM 2 interrupt handler: runs the frame routine on its own stack
;
; Entered by the 50 Hz frame interrupt (IM 2, I = $84: every byte of the vector table at #R$8400 is $85, so every vector
; is $8585). It swaps HL with the return address on the stack, stores the return address in the operand of the JP at
; #R$85B9 and the interrupted SP in the operand of the LD SP at #R$85B6, switches SP to the interrupt stack (#R$855F, which
; ends at $8585), pushes IX, HL, AF, BC, DE, IY and the alternate set, and calls the routine whose address is in
; #R$8504 through #R$85BC (JP (HL)). Afterwards it pops everything, enables interrupts and returns with LD SP and JP
; through the two patched operands. The interrupted code needs no stack room: the main loop runs the frame list with
; SP as a data pointer (#R$BDDB) and is interrupted in the middle of it.
; .
; The routine called is #R$85E2 while the game (or the intro room 0) runs, #R$A43D during the title and the controls
; menu, and the RET at #R$8719 before the game starts (#R$6000). One run of #R$85E2 takes about 5,500 to 7,300
; T-states, plus a burst of up to 9,000 for a beeper sound effect, of the 69,888 of a frame (observed with the
; simulator, frames 200-260 of tests/inputs/play.txt: interrupt accepted at T 19-28 of the frame, #R$A5DB reached at
; T 5,700-7,300).
; .
; Hardware: the IM 2 frame interrupt. Interrupts stay disabled until the EI at #R$85B5, so the frame routine is never
; interrupted. By reading the code (not seen): an interrupt accepted between that EI and the JP at #R$85B9 would store
; $85B9 as the return address and then hang on JP $85B9; it needs a run of the handler that ends exactly as the next
; frame starts.
; .
; O:All All registers are restored; the interrupted code goes on unaware
@label=InterruptHandler
c$8585 EX (SP),HL           ; HL = interrupted PC; the old HL is now on the stack
@isub=LD (SmcInterruptReturn+1),HL
 $8586 LD ($85BA),HL        ; return address: operand of the JP at $85B9
 $8589 POP HL               ; HL as it was
@isub=LD (SmcInterruptSP+1),SP
 $858A LD ($85B7),SP        ; interrupted SP: operand of the LD SP at $85B6
 $858E PUSH BC              ; no effect: writes BC below the interrupted SP, never read back (BC is pushed again at $8597)
 $858F DI                   ; (the CPU disabled interrupts when it accepted this one)
 $8590 LD SP,$8585          ; interrupt stack, #R$855F-$8584
 $8593 PUSH IX
 $8595 PUSH HL
 $8596 PUSH AF
 $8597 PUSH BC
 $8598 PUSH DE
 $8599 EXX                  ; switch to the alternate set, saved next
 $859A EX AF,AF'
 $859B PUSH IY
 $859D PUSH HL
 $859E PUSH AF
 $859F PUSH BC
 $85A0 PUSH DE
 $85A1 LD HL,($8504)        ; frame routine address
 $85A4 CALL $85BC           ; JP (HL): call the frame routine
 $85A7 POP DE
 $85A8 POP BC
 $85A9 POP AF
 $85AA POP HL
 $85AB POP IY
 $85AD EX AF,AF'
 $85AE EXX
 $85AF POP DE
 $85B0 POP BC
 $85B1 POP AF
 $85B2 POP HL
 $85B3 POP IX
 $85B5 EI                   ; the only EI of the frame; takes effect after the LD SP
@label=SmcInterruptSP
 $85B6 LD SP,$85B6          ; SMC: the operand is the interrupted SP, stored by the LD at $858A
@label=SmcInterruptReturn
*$85B9 JP $85B9             ; SMC: the operand is the interrupted PC, stored by the LD at $8586
